
Traceability Document: Merging Seattle Rental Property Registration and RRIO Datasets
1. Document Purpose
This traceability document outlines the complete process for merging two datasets:
Rental Property Registration snapshots (14 files, ~240k rows total, with details like units, owners, dates, and geo data).
RRIO historical records (13 files, ~215k rows total, with enforcement actions like violations, inspections, and private inspector flags).
The goal is to create a unified master file that tells the "whole story" of each address: registrations over time, lapses, private inspectors, violations, transfers, and other red flags. This enables analysis of patterns like uncollected penalties, delayed inspections, and landlord non-compliance.
This document is reproducible: It includes all steps, code snippets, assumptions, challenges, and rationale. You can recreate the merge by running the provided scripts in order.
2. Data Sources
Rental Property Registration Files (your cleaned versions):
Files: 14 CSV files named like "Rental_Property_Registration_20240611.csv", "Rental_Property_Registration_20240620MM.csv", etc.
Headers: REGISTRATIONNUMBER, UNITS, PROPERTYNAME, REGISTEREDDATE, EXPIRESDATE, STATUSCURRENT, ORIGINALADDRESS1, ORIGINALZIP, PROPERTYCONTACTNAME, LATITUDE, LONGITUDE.
Content: Snapshot of active registrations each pull date. Units, owners, dates; some have geo. Addresses sometimes funky (extra spaces, "SEATTLE" embedded).
Total rows after merge: 239,906 (deduped by REGISTRATIONNUMBER, keeping latest EXPIRESDATE).
RRIO Files (historical lists):
Files: 13 CSV files named like "RRIO_01012019_12312019_RecordList20251120.csv", "RRIO 2015-1016.csv", etc.
Headers: DATE, RECORDNUMBER, RECORDTYPE, PRIVATEINSPECTOR, STATUSCURRENT, REGISTRATIONEXPIRED, ORIGINALADDRESS1, (UNNAMED: 5 dropped as empty).
Content: Annual dumps of all actions (registrations, inspections, violations, private inspectors, expirations). Less detail than registrations, but shows the "story" (NOVs, delays, transfers).
Total rows after merge: 215,543 (filtered to valid RECORDNUMBER).
Input Files Location: All CSVs in one folder (e.g., C:\Users\stlca\stlca_data\stlca-dashboard\CITY_MVP\polars).
Output Files:
MASTER_REGISTRATIONS_V3.csv (from Phase 1).
MASTER_RRIO_Clean_fixed.csv (from Phase 2).
FINAL_MERGED_MASTER.csv (from Phase 3 — the overlay).
3. Process Overview
We merged in three phases to handle inconsistencies:
Phase 1: Merge all Rental Property Registration files into one master (dedup by REGISTRATIONNUMBER, sort by address).
Phase 2: Merge all RRIO files into one master (filter valid RECORDNUMBER, sort by address).
Phase 3: Overlay RRIO on registrations by matching on cleaned ORIGINALADDRESS1/address. Add flags from RRIO (e.g., had_private_inspector, had_violation_or_expiration) to each registration row. For addresses with multiple RRIO entries, aggregate (e.g., any private inspector = true, unique statuses seen as list).
This is staged to avoid schema mismatches (different column counts). All code is Python with Polars for efficiency.
4. Challenges and Rationale
Schema Differences: Registrations have 11 columns; RRIO have 7. Rationale: Add null columns in merge; use left join in overlay so registrations stay intact.
Address Matching: Funky spacing/"SEATTLE" embedded. Rationale: Clean with .str.replace(r"\s+", " ") and .str.replace("SEATTLE", "") in code, then upper-case for matching.
Duplicates: Addresses repeat across files/years (normal for history). Rationale: Dedup registrations by REGISTRATIONNUMBER (keep latest); aggregate RRIO per address (any/unique).
Empty Cells: Many blanks (e.g., PRIVATEINSPECTOR in some RRIO). Rationale: Polars handles nulls naturally; flags use .any() which treats null as false.
Encoding Issues: Some files had invalid bytes (0xc3). Rationale: Use 'utf-8-sig' to strip BOM; you resaved the two bad ones.
Filtering: Only keep valid XXX-XXXXXXX numbers. Rationale: Skips junk/footers.
Dates: MM/DD/YYYY. Rationale: Parse with .str.to_date("%m/%d/%Y").
Why Polars?: Handles large data fast, doesn't crash on blanks/duplicates if configured right. It "understands" your data once we tell it to ignore schema diffs with how="vertical_relaxed".
5. Reproducibility Steps
Place all 14 registration and 13 RRIO CSVs in one folder.
Run Phase 1 script (merge_registrations_phase1_v3.py) to get MASTER_REGISTRATIONS_V3.csv.
Run Phase 2 script (merge_rrio_phase2_fixed.py) to get MASTER_RRIO_Clean_fixed.csv.
Run Phase 3 script (below) to overlay and get FINAL_MERGED_MASTER.csv.
To recreate: The code is self-contained; no external deps beyond Polars (pip install polars).
Phase 3 Code: Final Overlay Merge
This loads the two masters, cleans addresses, overlays RRIO flags on registrations, and saves the final merged master.
O per address
rrio_agg = rrio.group_by("address_clean").agg([
    pl.col("private_inspector").any().alias("had_private_inspector"),
    pl.col("status_current").str.contains("violation|nov|expired", ignore_case=True).any().alias("had_violation_or_expiration"),
    pl.col("record_type").unique().alias("rrio_types_seen"),
    pl.col("status_current").unique().alias("rrio_statuses_seen"),
    pl.col("snapshot_file").unique().alias("rrio_sources")
])

# Overlay on registrations (left join — all reg rows kept, add RRIO flags where match)
final_merged = reg.join(rrio_agg, on="address_clean", how="left")

# Drop temp clean column
final_merged = final_merged.drop("address_clean")

# Save
final_merged.write_csv("FINAL_MERGED_MASTER.csv")

print(f"DONE — Final merged {final_merged.height:,} rows → FINAL_MERGED_MASTER.csv")
print("\nQuick peek:")
print(final_merged.head(5))
print("\nAddresses with private inspectors:")
print(final_merged.filter(pl.col("had_private_inspector") == True).select("ORIGINALADDRESS1").unique().head(10))


